.\" mount.gitfs(8) manual page — semantics per rfc/0000-gitfs.md (Q1-Q21)
.TH MOUNT.GITFS 8 "2026-09-24" "gitfs 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount.gitfs \- mount a git repository as a read\-only FUSE filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount.gitfs
.RB [ options ]
.I repository
.I mountpoint
.br
.B mount
.BI \-t \ gitfs
.RB [ options ]
.I repository
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount.gitfs
mounts a local git repository (bare or non\-bare, sha1 or sha256) as a
.I read\-only
FUSE filesystem.
Every branch, tag, remote\-tracking ref, and commit is presented as a
directory containing the full snapshot of its tree, so any revision can be
browsed with ordinary tools
.RB ( ls ,
.BR cat ,
.BR grep ,
diff tools) without
.BR git\ checkout ,
.BR git\ worktree ,
or
.BR git\ archive .
.PP
The repository is never modified: all write operations fail with
.BR EROFS ,
and the mount is forcibly
.BR ro ", " nosuid ", " nodev
with
.BR default_permissions .
Object access is provided by libgit2; no
.B git
subprocesses are spawned and no network access is performed.
.SH INVOCATION
.B mount.gitfs
follows the
.BR mount (8)
helper convention
.RI ( mount.<type> )
and is installed in the system sbin directory, so it is invoked in one of
three equivalent ways:
.TP
.BI mount \-t \ gitfs \ repository \ mountpoint
.BR mount (8)
execs the helper with the helper\-contract short options
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-n ,
and
.B \-v
plus the
.B \-o
options string (see OPTIONS for the mapping; behavior verified
against util\-linux 2.42.3 libmount).
.B \-\-fake
is forwarded to the helper as
.BR \-f ;
.B \-r
and
.B \-w
are never forwarded as flags \(em libmount folds them into the
.B \-o
string as
.B ro
or
.B rw
\(em and a call without explicit read\-only intent (no
.B \-r
and no
.BR \-o\ ro )
always reaches the helper with
.B rw
already in its options string, so a plain
.B "mount \-t gitfs repo dir"
invokes
.BR "mount.gitfs \-o rw repo dir" ,
which the helper accepts with a warning (see
.B \-o
under OPTIONS).
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
entry
.RS
.nf
/path/to/repo  /mnt/gitfs  gitfs  ro,blob\-cache\-size=128  0  0
.fi
.RE
.IP
Generic VFS options (exec, auto, user, async, ...) are translated by
.BR mount (8)
itself and never reach the helper.
With util\-linux >= 2.35 a command\-line
.B \-o
augments the fstab options (the merged string may list the same key
twice); gitfs\-specific keys then follow last\-one\-wins ordering
(see OPTIONS).
.TP
.B mount.gitfs
.I repository mountpoint
Direct invocation; identical semantics.
.PP
Unmount with
.BR umount (8)
or
.BR fusermount3 (1).
.SH MOUNT LAYOUT
.TP
.I branch/
Local branches.
Branch names containing
.B /
(e.g.\&
.IR feature/foo )
are rendered as nested directories.
A branch whose ref does not peel to a commit (e.g.\& manually
pointed at a blob or tree with
.BR "git update\-ref" )
is not presented
.RB ( ENOENT ,
with a warning logged).
.TP
.I tag/
All tags, annotated and lightweight.
A tag that points directly to a tree or blob object is not presented
.RB ( ENOENT );
only commit targets are shown.
.TP
.I commit/
Arbitrary commits by full object id:
.I commit/<oid>
where
.I <oid>
is 40 lowercase hex chars (sha1) or 64 (sha256) \(em uppercase hex is
rejected with
.B ENOENT
\(em naming a commit object itself
\(em no peeling, no abbreviation.
Listing
.I commit/
always returns an empty directory (commits are not enumerable).
To resolve a short prefix, grep the
.I commits
listing file instead.
.TP
.I remote/
Remote\-tracking refs
.IR remote/<remote>/<branch> ,
read directly from
.IR refs/remotes/ .
The symbolic ref
.I refs/remotes/<remote>/HEAD
is hidden (access yields
.BR ENOENT );
a ref that does not peel to a commit is likewise not presented
.RB ( ENOENT ).
.TP
.I HEAD/
Snapshot of the current
.BR HEAD .
An unborn
.B HEAD
yields
.BR ENOENT ;
so does a HEAD pointing at a non\-commit object (same rule as
tags and branches).
.TP
.I commits
Read\-only text file listing the full oid of every commit reachable from
all refs
.B and
.BR HEAD
(every ref under
.I refs/
, including notes, stash, and replace namespaces \(em matching
.B "git \-\-no\-replace\-objects rev\-list \-\-all"
\(em plus detached\-HEAD\-only commits; refs that do not peel to a
commit, such as a lightweight tag on a blob or tree, are skipped with
a verbose log entry and are not an error), one per line, in deterministic
topological order
(GIT_SORT_TOPOLOGICAL; refs are pushed in sorted ref\-name order, HEAD
last, so the relative order of parallel commits is reproducible byte
for byte).
It is generated lazily on first
.BR open ;
before that
.B stat
reports size 0.
On the first
.B open
the generation runs a full revwalk: on huge repositories (millions of
commits) this can take seconds, and the open blocks for that long.
Each
.B open
pins one snapshot version for the lifetime of the file handle, so refs
changing mid\-read never corrupt a reader.
.TP
.I .gitfs.json
Mount metadata: format version, absolute repository path,
.B head
(ref name; full oid when detached; null when unborn),
mount timestamp, and the configured cache sizes in bytes
.RB ( cache.blob_bytes ,
.BR cache.tree_bytes ).
If the repository path contains bytes that are not valid UTF\-8, the
.I repository
field percent\-escapes them as
.BI % XX
(ASCII and valid multi\-byte sequences are kept verbatim) so the file
remains valid JSON; the escaping is not reversible (a literal
.BI % XX
already present in the path is indistinguishable from an escaped
byte).
It is an immutable snapshot taken at mount time and does not track later
HEAD movement \(em use
.I HEAD/
or
.B git\ rev\-parse
for live HEAD.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o \ option[,option...]
Comma\-separated options, the mount(8)\-style form of the long options
below.
The gitfs\-specific keys
.BI blob\-cache\-size= MiB
and
.BI tree\-cache\-size= MiB
(dash and underscore spellings are equivalent) have exactly the same
semantics and validation as the corresponding long options.
If the same key is given more than once (within one \-o string, across
fstab and command line \(em util\-linux >= 2.35 merges them \(em or
mixed with the long options in command\-line order), the last
occurrence wins, matching mount(8) conventions.
Keys synonymous with the hardcoded baseline
.RB ( ro ,
.BR nosuid ,
.BR nodev ,
.BR default_permissions ,
.BR use_ino )
are accepted as redundant no\-ops.
.B rw
is likewise accepted as a no\-op, with a stderr warning: libmount
unconditionally places
.B rw
in the helper's options string for a call without read\-only intent,
so rejecting it would break the plain
.B "mount \-t gitfs repo dir"
invocation (ro stays hardcoded and cannot be diluted; ntfs\-3g uses
the same convention).
The remaining reverse keys
.RB ( suid ,
.B dev )
are usage errors, so the baseline cannot be diluted.
.BI fsname= name
may override the baseline fsname (cosmetic only);
.BI subtype= x
is baseline\-protected and overriding it is a usage error, since the
.I /proc/mounts
type field and
.B "mount \-t gitfs"
matching depend on it.
All other keys are forwarded verbatim to the libfuse option parser
(e.g.\&
.BR kernel_cache ;
.B allow_other
requires
.B user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf );
unknown keys are rejected by libfuse (exit status 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-blob\-cache\-size \ MiB
Blob LRU cache limit in MiB (default 64).
A single blob larger than the limit bypasses the cache: it is looked up
and fully decompressed once at
.BR open ,
the decompressed block is pinned on the file handle for the lifetime
of the open, and
.B release
frees it, so a sequential read decompresses exactly once (nothing is
evicted to make room).
Only the object lookup is performed under gitfs's internal lock; the
full decompression runs outside it, so other requests on the mount are
not stalled while it runs.
The
.B open
itself still waits for the whole decompression (seconds for multi-GB
blobs), the same way a first
.I commits
.B open
waits for its revwalk.
Cache\-miss loads of cacheable blobs use the same split (lookup plus
double\-check under the lock, decompression outside it, recheck before
inserting), so the lock is never held across a whole\-blob decompression
however large the cache is; concurrent first loads of the same blob may
transiently decompress twice (the loser's block is discarded).
The oversize test itself \-\- and every reported blob
.BR st_size \-\-
comes from a header\-only ODB read
.RB ( git_odb_read_header ),
so
.B stat
never triggers a blob decompression, just as it never triggers the
.I commits
revwalk.
The memory footprint of oversized blobs is the sum of their sizes
across concurrently open handles.
The value must be a positive integer; 0, negative, or unparseable
values are usage errors (exit status 1); there is no artificial upper
cap (bounded only by available memory).
.TP
.BI \-\-tree\-cache\-size \ MiB
Total budget of the libgit2 object cache (trees and commits) in MiB
(default 256).
Validation is identical to
.BR \-\-blob\-cache\-size .
Tree/commit metadata is served from this separate internal cache and is
not governed by
.BR \-\-blob\-cache\-size ;
the two caches account independently and never evict each other.
.TP
.BR \-\-foreground
Run in the foreground (default: daemonize after mounting).
Long option only: the short
.B \-f
is reserved for the mount(8) helper contract (fake mode, below).
.TP
.B \-f
Fake mode \(em the form in which
.BR mount (8)'s
.B \-\-fake
is forwarded to helpers (libmount's exec_helper passes
.BR \-f ).
All arguments and options are fully validated, then the helper exits
with status 0 without mounting anything.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log path resolution, cache hits, and blob decompression events to
stderr (one line per full decompression, covering both oversized
open\-pin decompressions and cache\-miss LRU loads; a plain
.B "ls \-l"
produces no decompression lines since blob sizes are read from ODB
headers only).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-s
Accepted and ignored (no\-mtab / sloppy flags forwarded by
.BR mount (8)).
.TP
.B \-r ", " \-w
Never received from
.BR mount (8) :
libmount does not forward them as flags, it appends
.B ro
or
.B rw
to the
.B \-o
string instead (\-r / \-o ro are redundant no\-ops; \-o rw is a
warned no\-op, see above).
Passing them explicitly on a direct invocation is a usage error
(the filesystem is read\-only either way).
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-\-help
Print version / usage and exit.
.SH SEMANTICS
.SS Read\-only data plane
Every mutating syscall returns
.BR EROFS ;
the mount is
.B ro\ +\ nosuid\ +\ nodev
and the kernel performs standard permission checks
.RB ( default_permissions ).
.SS Extended attributes
No xattr operation is registered, so
.BR getxattr ,
.BR listxattr ,
and friends report
.B ENOTSUP
(including the
.I security.*
queries that statx issues under SELinux).
.SS File metadata
Directories are 0755, plain files 0644, executables 0755, symlinks 0777.
The synthetic files
.I commits
and
.I .gitfs.json
are mode 0444 (nlink 1); the root directory and the five entry
directories
.BR branch/ ,
.BR tag/ ,
.BR remote/ ,
.BR commit/ ,
and
.I HEAD/
report the mount time as mtime/ctime/atime (nlink 2).
.BR st_mtime ", " st_ctime ,
and
.B st_atime
of tree content are all the committer time of the underlying commit
(constant within a snapshot; atime never advances on read).
.I commits
carries its generation time
.RB ( mount
time until first generated, while
.B stat
still reports size 0; after a fingerprint rebuild, stat reports the
current cached version's time),
.I .gitfs.json
the mount time.
Blob
.B st_size
is obtained from a header\-only ODB read
.RB ( git_odb_read_header ),
never from a full decompression: stat'ing a directory that contains
multi\-GB blobs costs no more than a header lookup, just as getattr
never triggers the
.I commits
revwalk.
.PP
.B st_blocks
is always
.BI ceil ( st_size /512)
(512\-byte blocks; the synthetic files follow the same formula on
their content size),
.B st_blksize
is 4096, and
.B st_rdev
is 0.
libfuse does not derive st_blocks from st_size, and leaving it at its
default 0 would make
.B du
report zero blocks for every file.
.PP
.B st_ino
is a 64\-bit hash of the full path.
Hash collisions are resolved by a mount\-local registry, so two
different paths never share an inode; remount stability holds except
for the astronomically rare colliding paths (~3e\-8 per million
entries).
The registry registers paths lazily (only paths actually stat'ed or
listed) and never frees entries during the mount, so its memory grows
with the number of distinct paths touched and inode numbers are never
reused.
Equal inodes never imply hard links or shared content.
.SS Submodules
Rendered as an empty directory plus a sibling text file
.IR <name>\.gitfs\-submodule
(mode 0644) containing two lines,
.B url=<url>
and
.BI commit= <oid> ,
where the url comes from the
.I .gitmodules
blob at that commit (empty, with a warning, if absent).
.SS Symbolic links
readlink returns the raw blob content, truncated at the first embedded
NUL (kernel restriction, same as
.B "git checkout"
behavior); an empty blob yields an empty target.
Targets may point anywhere, including outside the snapshot.
.SS Object replacement
.B gitfs
does not honor
.IR refs/replace :
objects are always presented raw, matching
.B "git \-\-no\-replace\-objects"
semantics, so an oid maps to exactly one immutable object.
.SS Names
Tree entry names are passed through as raw bytes (non\-UTF\-8 names are
not rewritten); entries literally named
.B .
or
.B ..
are skipped.
All directory listings (root,
.BR branch/ ,
.BR tag/ ,
.BR remote/ ,
and tree directories, including the
.I .gitfs\-submodule
synthetic entries) are emitted in raw\-byte lexicographic order of each
path component (memcmp, locale\-independent); tree entries are
re\-sorted rather than following git's intrinsic tree order (where
directory names compare as if suffixed with '/').
.B statfs
reports zero free blocks and zero free inodes, so
.B df
shows the mount as 100% used;
.I f_blocks
reflects the local object database size (packs + loose objects,
alternates excluded).
.SS Live updates
.BR branch/ ", " tag/ ", and " remote/
listings reflect external repository changes (new commits, tags, deleted
branches) without remounting.
Kernel attribute and entry cache timeouts are pinned to 0 to avoid stale
entries.
Users who know the repository will not change during a mount may opt in
with
.BR \-o\ kernel_cache ,
which affects only data page caching (read/mmap); attribute and entry
timeouts remain 0, and a metadata staleness window requires additionally
passing
.B attr_timeout
or
.BR entry_timeout .
.SS Empty repositories
A repository with no refs and an unborn
.B HEAD
mounts successfully:
.IR branch/ ,
.IR tag/ ,
and
.I remote/
are empty directories,
.I HEAD/
yields
.BR ENOENT ,
.I commits
is an empty file (size 0), and
.I .gitfs.json
reports
.B head
as null.
.SS Concurrent repository maintenance
.B gitfs
neither locks the repository nor blocks external git commands.
If
.B "git gc"
or
.B "git prune"
rewrites packs or removes objects that a pending request still needs,
that request transiently fails with
.B ENOENT
(object gone) or
.B EIO
(pack invalidated mid\-read), and recovers once gc finishes;
.B gitfs
does not promise a stable object set for the lifetime of a mount.
If a first\-time
.I commits
generation hits this mid\-revwalk, that
.B open
fails with
.BR EIO ;
the partial buffer is not cached and the fingerprint is left untouched,
so the next
.B open
retries from scratch.
When transient errors are unacceptable, disable automatic gc for the
duration of the mount
.RB ( git\ config\ gc.auto\ 0 ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal unmount, or a successful fake run of
.B \-f
(arguments validated, nothing mounted).
.TP
.B 1
Usage error (bad arguments, forbidden
.B \-o
keys).
.TP
.B 2
Repository unreadable or not a git repository.
.TP
.B 3
Mount failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mount \-t gitfs ~/src/myproj /mnt/gitfs
mount \-\-fake \-t gitfs ~/src/myproj /mnt/gitfs   # forwarded as \-f: validate only
ls /mnt/gitfs/branch/main/src
grep ^0beec7b /mnt/gitfs/commits      # resolve a short prefix
cat /mnt/gitfs/commit/<full\-oid>/README.md
diff \-r /mnt/gitfs/tag/v1.0/src /mnt/gitfs/branch/main/src
fusermount3 \-u /mnt/gitfs            # or: umount /mnt/gitfs
.fi
.PP
/etc/fstab:
.nf
/path/to/repo  /mnt/gitfs  gitfs  ro,blob\-cache\-size=128,tree\-cache\-size=512  0  0
.fi
.SH SECURITY
The kernel enforces ownership and permission checks
.RB ( default_permissions )
and the mount is read\-only without suid or device nodes.
Symlink targets and file names are repository\-controlled data and are
passed through unmodified; do not browse mounts of untrusted
repositories with root or other privileged accounts.
The repository path is canonicalized with
.B realpath
before use.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR git (1),
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR mount.fuse3 (8)
.PP
Full design and semantics:
.I rfc/0000\-gitfs.md
and
.I docs/filesystem\-semantics.md
in the gitfs source repository.
